e and comparison are in reason. The existing bespoke attribution conflicts remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S118-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"be7248ecae7ca53dc12f03edd4e628d23ca6d0f79ee6d1af0a554c9d539fdf9f\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 permits fury alongside eating and A3 removes the need to decide about food.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's inability to think about food; A5 honors B4 verbally but adds no new practical accommodation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 identifies limited capacity and B4 resists being told to calm down, constraining A's help.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's furious toast restates A1's coexistence of anger and food without developing the support.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies toast and fury but no event or immediate task around them.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's toast phrase is compact and relevant, though it substitutes a slogan for further action.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 78\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You can be furious about the cancelled train and still have toast before we drive.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can't think about food.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll wrap it. You can decide when we're on the road.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Don't spend the drive telling me to calm down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Agreed. Directions only. The toast won't require a better attitude either.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A recognizes anger as valid and adapts food, timing, and conversational boundaries to make the next task manageable.\",\n      \"conf